WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.660
Will the data we encrypt today
still be safe in ten years?

00:00:04.005 --> 00:00:07.055
Quantum computers and
AI speed each other up.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.440
Four risks follow.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.180
First, harvest now, decrypt later.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:16.340
A hostile party may be recording
our encrypted traffic today.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.700
It then waits for a quantum
computer that can read it.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.390
If the data still matters in ten years,
the risk is today.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.500
Second, AI may find shortcuts.

00:00:27.005 --> 00:00:32.355
AI can spot patterns in encrypted data
and in implementations that people missed.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.510
This is not a known attack yet.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.370
It is a category of risk.

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:40.105
We cannot assume our encryption
is safe until we test it.

00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:43.213
Third, quantum and AI together.

00:00:43.488 --> 00:00:48.378
A large quantum computer breaks
RSA and ECC with Shor's algorithm,

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.218
with no help from AI.

00:00:50.688 --> 00:00:53.398
AES-256 stays strong.

00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:58.523
AI speeds up quantum research,
and finds implementation flaws faster.

00:01:00.497 --> 00:01:01.087
Fourth, migration lag.

00:01:01.332 --> 00:01:06.642
The Dutch AIVD says a useful quantum
computer is possible from 2030.

00:01:07.732 --> 00:01:11.522
Migration is long, because it touches
every system that holds secrets.

00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:15.467
Data encrypted today stays
exposed if we switch late.

00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:17.727
What good looks like.

00:01:18.352 --> 00:01:19.502
A cryptographic inventory.

00:01:20.007 --> 00:01:21.817
The NIST post-quantum standards,

00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:23.037
FIPS 203,

00:01:23.707 --> 00:01:24.357
204

00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:25.717
and 205.

00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:28.297
Hybrid post-quantum key exchange.

00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:31.092
Hardware security modules hold the keys.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.707
And the ability to switch
algorithms quickly.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.840
A suggested first step.

00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:39.755
Ask for an inventory of
where we use encryption,

00:01:40.265 --> 00:01:43.295
plus a plan, by the end of 2027.

00:01:43.565 --> 00:01:46.755
Aim to protect the most
sensitive data by 2030.

00:01:47.305 --> 00:01:48.855
Finish by 2035.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.960
That is a suggested timeline,

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:52.680
not a legal deadline.

00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:57.353
The data we encrypt today
will outlive both threats.

00:01:57.713 --> 00:01:59.223
Start the migration now.

